{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>So Avram went, etc.</b> The entire verse seems superfluous, for it says shortly after, in the sequence of narrating the journey (Genesis 12:5): ״וַיִּקַּח אַבְרָם״ etc. ״וַיֵּצְאוּ לָלֶכֶת״ etc." "<b>However,</b> the intent of the verse is to make known the belovedness of Avraham, that he did not delay even a single hour; rather, as soon as the word of HaShem ״לֶךְ לְךָ״ was complete, immediately ״וַיֵּלֶךְ אַבְרָם״, and he was not delayed for any reason, and he left his father and his birthplace. And the import of the כ in כַּאֲשֶׁר is to indicate the time — that it was immediately after HaShem had spoken to him. And the verse further makes known that Lot, on account of his cleaving to Avraham, went with him — meaning, not on account of fulfilling the word of HaShem, but rather he saw him going and went with him. And the intent of this disclosure is that even though Avraham was shrewd to hasten to depart, so that no one from his birthplace and his father's house would join up with him, this particular device availed him nothing, and Lot went with him." "Further, it may intend as follows: Since HaShem promised him many beneficial assurances when he would go from his land, etc., if so, then even though he went, the onlooker might suppose that no merit should be credited to him for this — for even the lightest of the light, upon seeing all the benefits, would hurry to travel. Therefore the verse makes known the righteousness of Avraham, that his going was not on account of the stated promises but rather to do the word of HaShem. And note carefully that it says <b>כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר</b> and not ״כַּאֲשֶׁר אָמַר״, as it began at the beginning of the parashah ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳״ — to confirm our point. And the principle is: you should know that wherever Scripture uses the term דִּבּוּר it indicates a harsh matter, whereas אֲמִירָה is soft. And here, at the beginning of the parashah, it used ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳״ because the entire matter was for Avraham's benefit; but regarding Avraham's deed it said ״כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר״ — meaning that he did the thing on account of a royal decree upon him and not for the benefit that would accrue to him." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to make known that Avraham went כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר — as HaShem had spoken to him immediately before. And what was said in the word of HaShem? ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר אַרְאֶךָּ״ — He did not inform him of the place to which he would come, and even so he went from his land and relied on what HaShem would make known to him whenever He willed, without thinking to say: How shall I leave the city before we know the place? And this is a great trial. And afterwards the verse makes known the taking along of those who were with him — that HaShem did not test them in this matter, but rather informed them of the place and afterwards they departed. And this is what it says in the verse that follows this one: <b>וַיִּקַּח אַבְרָם</b> etc. <b>וַיֵּצְאוּ לָלֶכֶת אַרְצָה כְּנָעַן</b>, and the verse had no need to state that HaShem informed him of the place, for it is self-evident."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:47.353460177Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}